[32m+[m[32m- **6.12.0** - Per-company WhatsApp bot menu, F1 (Alembic `wb1_whatsapp_bot_config` on `pz1_provisioning_zone`, additive; downgrade drops the table). **Schema:** new `whatsapp_bot_config` (`company_id` FK company ON DELETE CASCADE, unique index `uq_whatsapp_bot_config_company`; `enabled` default true, `welcome_text`, `menu_button_text` default `'Ver opciones'`, `options` JSON default `[]`); model `WhatsAppBotConfig` (`models/whatsapp_bot.py`). **New** `schemas/whatsapp_bot.py`: `WhatsAppBotConfigIn` / `WhatsAppBotConfigOut` and the option union discriminated by `type` — `INFO` (`text`), `CREATE_TASK` (`job_kind` from `TaskJobKind`, `task_name`, `requires_client` default true, `confirmation_text`) and `BUILTIN` (`flow` in `BUILTIN_FLOWS`: aumento, falla, factura, instalacion, agente, resincronizar). WhatsApp list limits are enforced: 1–10 options, title ≤ 24, description ≤ 72, button ≤ 20, ids `^[a-z0-9_]{1,40}$` and unique, unknown fields refused. `default_whatsapp_bot_config()` returns the bot's current menu, used when a company has no row or `enabled` is false. Consumers need not change; backend-erp (config endpoints) and whatsapp-bot-gateway (reading the menu) adopt it in the same F1 cycle. New tests `test_whatsapp_bot_config.py` and `pg/test_wb1_pg.py` (server defaults, one row per company, company FK + cascade, idempotent round trip).[m
 - **6.11.3** - Visible secrets and a `last` filter. `PlaybookSecret.visible` (default false): a visible secret may be a plain, non-sensitive output (`{{secret.<key>}}` with `sensitive` false or unset), for values the office must read on the run later such as a generated customer Wi-Fi password; it is still generated per run, encrypted on the run and masked in logs. A non-visible secret output stays always sensitive (the check moved from `PlaybookOutput` to `PlaybookDefinition`, which knows the secrets). Template filter `last:N` (the last N characters: `{{cpe.serial | last:4}}`). No schema change.[m
 - **6.11.2** - DEPROVISION runs top-down too, root → leaf: core router → OLT → CPE (founder decision 2026-10-19, CSR's deprovision order: cut the PPPoE session first, then the OLT, then the ACS). `order_for_purpose` reverses `resolved.steps` for `TOP_DOWN_PURPOSES` = {ACTIVATION, DEPROVISION}. SUSPENSION, REACTIVATION and custom purposes keep leaf → root. Rollback stays reverse configuration order. No schema change.[m
 - **6.11.1** - ACTIVATION runs top-down again, root → leaf: core router → OLT → CPE (founder decision 2026-10-17). The service has to exist on the MikroTik and the OLT before the CPE's last steps (OMCI, telnet or ACS) can work. `order_for_purpose` returns `resolved.steps` reversed for ACTIVATION only. REACTIVATION, SUSPENSION, DEPROVISION and custom purposes keep 6.7.2's leaf → root order. Phases stay phase-major; whole-run rollback still runs in reverse configuration order, so an ACTIVATION rolls back CPE first, core last. The CPE session probe is position-based and unchanged. No schema change; runs already created keep their snapshotted plan.[m
